 # DINER
 
-Also called: Depth-aware Image-based NEural Radiance fields
+Also called: DINER (Depth-aware Image-based NEural Radiance fields), Depth-aware Image-based NEural Radiance fields
 
 ## Overview
-DINER is a method for novel view synthesis from a sparse set of RGB input views (typically four) with high relative disparity, predicting depth and feature maps to guide the reconstruction of a volumetric scene representation [S1]. It solves the problem of synthesis artifacts and geometry estimation failure that occurs in standard image-based NeRFs when source cameras are far apart, by leveraging dense depth maps instead of sparse landmarks or implicit correspondence [S1]. Unlike siblings like SfMNeRF or DASNeRF which may rely on different geometric priors or optimization strategies, DINER explicitly conditions the neural radiance field on the deviation between sample locations and estimated depth, and employs depth-guided sampling to improve efficiency [S1].
+DINER is a method for novel view synthesis from sparse input views that leverages estimated dense depth maps to guide the reconstruction of a volumetric scene representation [S1]. Unlike standard image-based NeRFs that rely on implicit correspondence search or sparse landmarks, DINER uses a state-of-the-art depth estimator to provide dense geometric priors, enabling the processing of input views with high relative distances [S1]. It sets itself apart by conditioning the neural radiance field on the deviation between sample locations and estimated depth, and by employing depth-guided sampling to concentrate samples near surfaces, thereby improving sampling efficiency and synthesis quality [S1].
 
